      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place the pork shoulder in a stockpot with 2 litres of cold water. Bring to the boil and skim off all the foam."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the onion, garlic, and ginger. Reduce heat to a gentle simmer. Cook for 60 to 75 minutes until the pork is cooked through."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the pork and set aside to cool. Strain the broth through a sieve. Return to the pot."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Season the broth with fish sauce and sugar. Taste and adjust. It should be clear and lightly sweet and savoury."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Slice the cooked pork thinly. Add the prawns to the simmering broth and cook for 2 to 3 minutes until just pink."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Divide the cooked noodles between bowls. Top with sliced pork, a few prawns, and spring onion."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Ladle over the very hot broth. Serve with bean sprouts, coriander, lime wedges, and chilli on the side."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The broth is cloudy and grey.",
              "cause": "The water was not skimmed properly at the start.",
              "fix": "Always bring the pork to the boil in fresh cold water and skim off all the foam before adding aromatics. Simmer, do not boil."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The broth tastes flat and thin.",
              "cause": "The pork bones were used alone without enough simmering time.",
              "fix": "Add a handful of dried shrimp or a teaspoon of fish sauce at the start of cooking for extra depth and sweetness."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The noodles absorb all the broth and become stodgy.",
              "cause": "The noodles were added to the broth bowl too early.",
              "fix": "Keep the noodles and broth separate until serving. Assemble each bowl just before eating."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Hủ tiếu is a beloved noodle soup of the south, especially in Ho Chi Minh City and the Mekong Delta. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "It shows the influence of Chinese and Cambodian cooking on southern Vietnamese food. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The dish is eaten at all hours of the day from pushcart vendors. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "In the Mekong Delta, the broth is slightly sweeter than the city version. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "It is one of the most versatile noodle soups in Vietnam, with toppings varying by region and vendor."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
